From b99bce1c20872c92d0a3a3b1e60bdcbca0a97727 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:51:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP: Remove a STA that associated with another AP of the ESS

A STA that reassociates to another AP of the ESS does not tell the AP it
leaves (with FT, it never talks to it again), and that AP keeps it
associated until the inactivity timer or excessive missing ACKs remove
it. Neither happens while the STA still acknowledges some of the frames
sent to it on the old AP's channel, as a STA that visits the channel for
peer-to-peer links does: the AP keeps transmitting to it what is
addressed to it, its other STAs' frames and group frames sent as
unicast, every success resets the inactivity, and the failures never run
long enough for a kickout. The frames are lost, for as long as anything
is addressed to the STA. Seen with HomePods of a stereo pair, which
visit the old AP's channel for AWDL: one speaker's audio went to its
partner's stale association on the old AP.

The new AP's kernel sends a Layer 2 Update frame for each association
(cfg80211_send_layer2_update()), so that bridges and switches learn
where the STA is; the old AP receives it from the distribution system.
Add prune_roamed_sta: on a Layer 2 Update frame received on a wired
bridge port for one of its associated STAs, the AP removes the STA
unless it receives a frame from it within two seconds, which a STA that
left does not send and one still here does. A forged frame therefore
cannot remove an active STA; an idle one reassociates when it next
transmits. The STA is not sent a Deauthentication frame.

The frame is read on an ETH_P_ALL packet socket that ignores outgoing
frames, with a filter that passes only the Layer 2 Update frame: a
socket for one protocol gets a frame received on a bridge port only as
received on the bridge, once the bridge has consumed it on the port.

Tested with mac80211_hwsim: two APs on different channels whose bridges
are joined by a veth pair and a STA roaming between them. The old AP
removes the STA two seconds after it roams and keeps it with the option
off; the new AP ignores its own frame; a forged frame for an active STA
is refused, and one for an idle STA removes it until it reassociates.

Signed-off-by: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
